<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch02_P02_Follow_the_picture</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Follow the picture proof above with \(x = {@aa@}\).</p>
<p>(a) After the strip has been moved, the pieces form a rectangle. It measures</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] by [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) The proposition \(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\) is true for every real number \(x\), but the picture cannot always be drawn. Give an example of a value of \(x\) for which the picture cannot be drawn.</p>
<div>\(x =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) Which statement about the picture is correct?</p>
<div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>Type fractions as <code>1/2</code> and square roots as <code>sqrt(2)</code>.</p>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Follow the four steps of the picture with \(x = {@aa@}\).</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Start with a square {@aa@} by {@aa@}. Its area is {@aa^2@}.</p></li>
<li><p>Cut a 1 by 1 square from one corner. What is left has area \({@aa^2@} - 1 = {@aa^2 - 1@}\).</p></li>
<li><p>Cut off the strip along the top. It is {@aa - 1@} long and 1 high. Turn it and place it against the side.</p></li>
<li><p>The pieces form a rectangle {@tL@} by {@tS@}, with area \({@tL@} \times {@tS@} = {@tL*tS@}\), the same as before.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>The same moves work for any \(x \gt 1\), so the picture proves \(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\) for every real number \(x \gt 1\). For \(x \le 1\) the length \(x - 1\) is zero or negative and there is no picture; for example, at \(x = 0\) there is nothing to draw. Nothing in the picture needs \(x\) to be a whole number. The algebraic proof covers every real \(x\).</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
